Stop the posix fbtl losing a nonblocking write when the aio queue fills

Not an upstream backport, like the MPICH patches beside it are in part: a local fix
carried ahead of upstream. It is reported -- open-mpi/ompi#14278 for the defect and
open-mpi/ompi#14279 for this patch, three commits against `main`, whose source is
identical to what is below. Every file touched is byte-identical between `main` and
the ABI-branch commit install-openmpi.sh pins, which is why one patch serves both.
Drop this file once the PR lands and the pinned commit carries it; until then
whichever way review reshapes it, reshape this too rather than letting the two
drift. It is also what removed the `openmpi/*/darwin/*/*  f08  i_fcoll_test` line
from suite/mpich-suite-xfail.txt. MISSING.md, "OpenMPI on macOS: a nonblocking
collective write is lost when the aio queue fills", has the measurements; this is
the short version and the reasoning behind each hunk.

`f08/io/i_fcoll_test` writes a 32³ integer array through an `MPI_Type_create_darray`
view with `MPI_File_iwrite_all`, reads it back with `MPI_File_iread_all`, and finds
every element zero, after

    mca_fbtl_posix_ipwritev: error in aio_write():  Resource temporarily unavailable
    mca_fbtl_posix_ipreadv: error in aio_read(): errno 35 Resource temporarily unavailable

once per process. `MPI_File_iwrite_all` returns `MPI_SUCCESS` and so does
`MPI_Wait`.

**Where the number comes from.** `mca_fbtl_posix_module_init` sizes
`ompi_fbtl_posix_max_prd_active_reqs` -- how many `aio_write`s to keep in flight
at once -- from `sysconf(_SC_AIO_MAX)`. On Darwin that reports `kern.aiomax`, the
limit across every process on the machine, 90 by default; a single process is held
to `kern.aioprocmax`, 16. So the batch is 5.6x what the process is allowed, and
the seventeenth `aio_write` of the first batch returns EAGAIN. The patch reads the
per-process sysctl on Darwin, and adds an `fbtl_posix_max_aio_reqs` MCA parameter
so the value is reachable at all: today nothing but `sysconf` ever assigns it, so
an environment where the system's answer is wrong has no recourse.

**Why the retry cannot retry.** `fbtl_posix_ipwritev` already tries ten times per
request with `mca_common_ompio_progress()` in between, which is the fix for
open-mpi/ompi#8368, "Exceeding the max. number of pending aio requests on MacOS",
closed in the 4.1 series. It cannot help. A slot is released by `aio_return`, not
by the operation completing -- sixteen requests that have all finished still refuse
a seventeenth, and accept it the moment they are reaped -- and the only caller of
`aio_return` is `mca_fbtl_posix_progress`, which `mca_common_ompio_progress` reaches
through `req->req_progress_fn`, assigned *after* the loop. Nothing in the process
can reap this request's own operations while the loop runs.

So the retry is replaced by back-pressure. `mca_fbtl_posix_post_reqs` posts what
the queue will take and reports where it stopped; the active window becomes what
was accepted rather than what was wanted, and the remainder waits for a later
progress call. That needs one change in `mca_fbtl_posix_progress`: the next batch
goes out when every request in the active window is accounted for, `lcount ==
prd_last_active_req - prd_first_active_req`, rather than when a whole
`prd_req_chunks` of them are. The old test assumed every window is a full chunk,
which is exactly what a short window is not; comparing against the width of the
window handles a short one, and an empty one -- left when the queue would take
none at all -- reduces to the same expression and retries on the next call.

**Why the loss was silent.** Two things, both fixed here:

- `mca_common_ompio_file_iwrite` and `..._iread` call the fbtl as a statement and
  never look at what it returns. A request whose fbtl call failed carries no
  progress function, and `mca_common_ompio_progress` cannot tell that from a
  parent request with no subrequests left outstanding, so it completes the request
  with a status nobody has written: `MPI_Wait` reports `MPI_SUCCESS` and
  `MPI_Get_count` reports 0. These two now set `MPI_ERR_IO` and complete the
  request themselves. Note `MPI_File_iwrite_all` is where this is reached from --
  no fcoll component implements `fcoll_file_iwrite_all`, so
  `mca_common_ompio_file_iwrite_all` falls back to its own "WE fake it with
  individual non-blocking I/O operations" branch, and the io array is as
  fragmented as the file view.
- The error paths freed `prd_aio.aio_reqs` with operations still outstanding
  against those control blocks, and reaped none of them. POSIX requires an aiocb
  to stay valid until its operation completes, and an unreaped request costs the
  process a queue slot for the rest of its life -- so one EAGAIN disabled
  nonblocking file I/O for the process entirely, which is why the test read back
  nothing rather than the sixteen blocks the write had left behind.
  `mca_fbtl_posix_drain_reqs` waits for and reaps whatever was posted before the
  control blocks go away.

Not touched, deliberately: the two `fbtl_ipwritev`/`fbtl_ipreadv` calls in the
*blocking* write and read paths discard their return value the same way, and the
partial-completion re-post inside `mca_fbtl_posix_progress` still gives up on a
failed `aio_write` with the request marked EINPROGRESS and nothing in flight. Both
are reachable only from a genuine I/O error rather than from a full queue, neither
is what this defect is, and the second needs the lock dance rethought. They are in
MISSING.md as known and unfixed.

Measured on this repository's macOS 26 / arm64, `kern.aioprocmax` 16, against
Open MPI 6.1.0a1 at the ABI branch commit the installer pins. `f08/io`, `f90/io`
and `f77/io` all go from three failures to two, the one that goes being
`i_fcoll_test`; the two that remain, `c2f2ciof90` and `c2f90multio` and their f77
names, fail identically with and without this patch. Putting the bug back --
reverting, rebuilding, rerunning -- makes both reproducers in
bug-ompi-aio-eagain/ fail again with the original message, which is what earns the
claim. The corresponding openmpi/*/darwin/15/* variants are inferred rather than
measured: `kern.aioprocmax` is 16 there too, and nothing in the fix is
version-specific, but no macOS 15 machine was available to run it on.

